= Behavior — Technical =

'''Behavior''' is a system-level pattern of activity used in biology, psychology, neuroscience, ethology, behavior analysis, and engineering. The concept is scientifically central but definitionally heterogeneous. A technical analysis should therefore specify the unit, boundary, temporal scale, observability, and operational criteria before measurement.

Levitis, Lidicker and Freund found substantial disagreement among behavioral biologists and proposed a definition centered on internally coordinated actions or inactions of whole organisms or groups in response to internal or external stimuli, excluding changes more readily treated as development.<ref name="levitis">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2760923/ Levitis, D. A., Lidicker, W. Z. Jr., and Freund, G. (2009), “Behavioural biologists don't agree on what constitutes behaviour.”]</ref>


A technical account of behavior connects observable action to [[Sensory processing]], internal state, and environmental contingencies rather than reducing behavior to any one level. Learning and [[Memory]] can alter response probabilities, while [[Adaptation (systems)]] provides a broader systems vocabulary for changes that improve performance under specified conditions.

== State-space representation ==

Let organism state at time <math>t</math> be

<math>x_t=(x_t^{internal},x_t^{external}).</math>

Behavioral action can be represented as

<math>a_t=\pi(x_t,h_t),</math>

where <math>h_t</math> is relevant history and <math>\pi</math> a behavioral policy.

The next state becomes

<math>x_{t+1}=F(x_t,a_t,\epsilon_t),</math>

where <math>\epsilon_t</math> represents uncontrolled environmental variation.

This formulation makes behavior dynamic and reciprocal: actions alter future conditions.

== Operational definition ==

Suppose the researcher studies “aggression.”

A usable operational definition might identify events such as:

* bite;
* charge;
* threat display;
* chase.

Without explicit criteria, observers can import subjective judgments.

Operational definitions should state:

* onset;
* offset;
* inclusion;
* exclusion;
* ambiguous cases.

== Unit of analysis ==

Behavior can be defined at several levels:

* individual organism;
* dyad;
* group;
* colony;
* artificial agent.

Mixing levels creates category errors.

A neural spike is usually not classified as the organism's behavior.

A flock turn can be a group-level behavioral pattern even though no bird individually executes “the flock turn.”

The unit must be explicit.

== Temporal scale ==

Behavior can be sampled across milliseconds, seconds, hours, seasons, or lifetimes.

Observed variable depends on time resolution.

At fine scale, locomotion may be a sequence of motor events.

At coarse scale, the same episode is “migration.”

A behavioral ontology should therefore define temporal granularity.

== Tinbergen framework ==

Tinbergen distinguished four biological questions:<ref name="tinbergen">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1439-0310.1963.tb01161.x Tinbergen, N. (1963), “On aims and methods of Ethology.”]</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
! Question !! Target
|-
| Mechanism || Immediate causal processes
|-
| Ontogeny || Development across the lifetime
|-
| Function || Consequences for survival or reproduction
|-
| Phylogeny || Evolutionary history
|}

These explanatory dimensions should not be substituted for one another.

== Proximate mechanism ==

A mechanistic model can include sensory input <math>s_t</math>, internal state <math>z_t</math>, neural transformation <math>N</math>, and motor output <math>a_t</math>:

<math>a_t=N(s_t,z_t).</math>

Mechanism can be probed through:

* lesions;
* stimulation;
* pharmacology;
* electrophysiology;
* imaging;
* genetic manipulation.

Behavior alone rarely identifies mechanism uniquely.

== Development ==

Let behavioral parameter <math>\theta(t)</math> vary with age.

A developmental model might be

<math>\theta(t)=F(genotype,experience,environment,t).</math>

Developmental explanation includes maturation, learning, hormonal change, and environmental history.

Cross-sectional age differences are weaker evidence than longitudinal developmental observation because cohorts can differ.

== Function ==

A functional hypothesis predicts consequences for fitness.

If behavior <math>a</math> changes expected reproductive success,

<math>\Delta W=E[W\mid a]-E[W\mid a'],</math>

then functional analysis asks whether <math>\Delta W</math> is positive under the relevant ecological context.

Observed prevalence is not proof of adaptation.

Drift, constraint, pleiotropy, or historical mismatch can also explain traits.

== Phylogeny ==

Comparative behavioral evolution requires accounting for shared ancestry.

Species are not independent observations.

Phylogenetic comparative methods model covariance induced by evolutionary history.

A behavioral correlation across species can disappear after phylogenetic correction.

== Innate–learned decomposition ==

The binary distinction is often misleading.

Phenotype can be represented schematically as

<math>B=F(G,E,G\times E,D),</math>

where <math>G</math> is genetic variation, <math>E</math> environmental variation, and <math>D</math> developmental history.

“Genetic” does not mean fixed.

“Environmental” does not mean freely learned.

The interaction can be nonlinear.

== Reaction norms ==

For genotype <math>g</math>, behavioral phenotype across environment <math>e</math> can be written

<math>B_g(e).</math>

Different genotypes can have different reaction norms.

This formalizes context-dependent expression rather than assigning one fixed behavioral value to a genotype.

== Learning curves ==

Performance over trials can be modeled, for example, as

<math>P(n)=P_{\infty}-(P_{\infty}-P_0)e^{-kn}.</math>

Such a curve describes change but does not identify the learning mechanism.

Alternative models should be compared.

Learning can also produce nonmonotonic performance.

== Reinforcement learning analogy ==

Behavior analysis and computational reinforcement learning both study consequences of actions, but the frameworks are not identical.

A computational agent may update action value:

<math>Q(s,a)\leftarrow Q(s,a)+\alpha[r+\gamma\max_{a'}Q(s',a')-Q(s,a)].</math>

Animal learning can be compared with such models.

A good fit does not prove the nervous system literally implements the algorithm.

== Ethogram design ==

Let behavioral categories be

<math>C=\{c_1,\ldots,c_k\}.</math>

Categories should be:

* operationally defined;
* mutually interpretable;
* sufficient for the research question;
* feasible to observe.

Mutual exclusivity is not always required because an organism can simultaneously locomote and vocalize.

The coding scheme should specify whether overlap is allowed.

== Frequency ==

For observation duration <math>T</math>, event rate is

<math>\lambda=N/T.</math>

Counts can be modeled with Poisson or negative-binomial models when assumptions fit.

Overdispersion suggests heterogeneity or temporal clustering.

== Duration ==

For episodes with durations <math>d_i</math>:

<math>\bar d=\frac{1}{n}\sum_i d_i.</math>

Total time allocation can be expressed

<math>p_c=\frac{T_c}{T}.</math>

Duration distributions are often skewed, so medians or survival models can be more appropriate than means.

== Latency ==

Latency from stimulus to response is a time-to-event variable.

Censoring occurs when no response appears before observation ends.

Survival analysis can handle such censoring better than assigning arbitrary maximum latency values.

== Sequence analysis ==

Let behavioral sequence be

<math>S=(b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_T).</math>

Transition probabilities can be estimated:

<math>P_{ij}=P(b_{t+1}=j\mid b_t=i).</math>

Markov models can characterize local sequence structure.

Higher-order history may be needed if behavior depends on more than the previous state.

== Hidden-state models ==

Observed behavior can arise from latent states.

A hidden Markov model includes latent state <math>z_t</math>, transition probability

<math>P(z_t\mid z_{t-1})</math>,

and emission probability

<math>P(b_t\mid z_t).</math>

This can model unobserved states such as foraging mode or vigilance.

The latent state is a model construct, not a directly observed mental state.

== Sampling schemes ==

Common schemes include:

'''Focal sampling''' — follow one individual.

'''Scan sampling''' — record states across many individuals at intervals.

'''All-occurrence sampling''' — record every occurrence of selected rare behavior.

Sampling design changes detectability.

Rare short events are systematically underestimated by coarse scan intervals.

== Detection probability ==

Let true event probability be <math>p</math> and detection probability be <math>d</math>.

Observed event probability is roughly

<math>p_{obs}=pd.</math>

Comparisons across habitats or treatments are biased when <math>d</math> differs.

Observation conditions should be standardized or modeled.

== Inter-rater reliability ==

For categorical coding, Cohen's kappa is

<math>\kappa=\frac{p_o-p_e}{1-p_e},</math>

where <math>p_o</math> is observed agreement and <math>p_e</math> expected chance agreement.

Kappa has prevalence-related limitations.

Reliability statistics should be interpreted with raw agreement and category frequencies.

== Observer bias ==

Observer expectations can influence coding.

Controls include:

* blinded condition labels;
* automated scoring;
* preregistered categories;
* independent coders;
* randomized video order.

Automated classifiers can also be biased.

They require validation against representative ground truth.

== Repeated measures ==

Behavioral observations from one individual are not independent.

If animal <math>i</math> contributes repeated observations <math>y_{ij}</math>, a mixed model can include individual random effect:

<math>y_{ij}=\beta_0+\beta_1x_{ij}+u_i+\epsilon_{ij}.</math>

Ignoring repeated structure creates pseudoreplication and inflated precision.

== Social dependence ==

Members of one group can influence each other.

Treating every group member as independent can underestimate uncertainty.

Hierarchical models can include group-level random effects or explicit network dependence.

== Social network analysis ==

Let animals be nodes and interactions edges.

Weighted adjacency

<math>A_{ij}</math>

can encode interaction frequency.

Centrality metrics describe network structure.

High centrality does not automatically imply dominance, influence, or fitness.

Those interpretations require separate validation.

== Collective behavior ==

Local interaction rules can generate group patterns.

A generic model:

<math>v_i(t+1)=F(v_i(t),N_i(t),environment).</math>

Flocking can arise from attraction, alignment, and repulsion rules.

Emergent group pattern does not imply centralized control.

== Decision thresholds ==

A behavioral response can follow threshold model

<math>a=1\quad if\quad z>\theta.</math>

Observed response probability depends both on sensory evidence <math>z</math> and decision criterion <math>\theta</math>.

Therefore a behavioral detection threshold is not identical to a sensory receptor threshold.

This distinction is central to psychophysics.

== Signal detection theory ==

In a detection task, behavior can be decomposed into sensitivity and criterion.

Sensitivity:

<math>d'=Z(H)-Z(F),</math>

where <math>H</math> is hit rate and <math>F</math> false-alarm rate.

Two observers can have equal sensory sensitivity but different response biases.

Behavior alone must be interpreted through the decision model.

== Homeostatic control ==

Behavior can regulate internal variables.

Let deviation from set region be

<math>e_t=x^*-x_t.</math>

Behavioral action can reduce error:

<math>a_t=K e_t.</math>

Real biological regulation can involve multiple set regions, predictive control, learning, and allostasis.

The control-theoretic analogy should not be treated as a complete physiological model.

== Behavioral ecology ==

Behavioral ecology links behavior to ecological fitness consequences.

Optimization models can predict strategies under assumptions about:

* resource distribution;
* predation;
* competition;
* energetic cost.

Observed deviation from optimum can reveal constraints, incorrect assumptions, or changing environments.

Optimality is hypothesis, not presumption.

== Human behavior models ==

Human behavior research must account for:

* social norms;
* institutions;
* culture;
* language;
* strategic interaction;
* explicit beliefs.

A simple biological stimulus-response model can be insufficient.

Human experiments also require consent, privacy protection, and appropriate ethical review.

== Digital behavioral data ==

Digital traces such as clicks, location history, or platform activity can measure behavior at scale.

They are platform-mediated samples.

Missing users, ranking algorithms, interface design, and logging rules alter the observed behavior.

Digital exhaust is not neutral observation.

== Computational agents ==

An artificial agent can be defined by state <math>s</math>, action set <math>A</math>, policy <math>\pi(a\mid s)</math>, and transition dynamics.

Its behavior is operationally measurable.

Similarity to biological behavioral statistics does not establish shared cognition or consciousness.

== Mechanistic underdetermination ==

Let two mechanisms <math>M_1</math> and <math>M_2</math> produce identical behavioral distribution:

<math>P(B\mid M_1)=P(B\mid M_2).</math>

Behavioral data alone cannot distinguish them.

Additional neural, physiological, perturbational, or developmental evidence is needed.

== Anthropomorphism ==

Anthropomorphism attributes human-like mental states from superficial behavioral resemblance.

It can generate useful hypotheses but should not serve as evidence.

A rigorous interpretation asks what alternative mechanism could produce the same observable action.

== Replication and generalization ==

Behavior depends on context.

A result should be tested across:

* individuals;
* environments;
* populations;
* experimenters;
* time.

Laboratory reproducibility does not guarantee ecological validity.

Field observation does not guarantee causal identification.

Both methods can complement one another.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Behavior is an observation target, not a transparent window into mechanism, function, or subjective experience.

A defensible chain is:

<pre>
operational definition
→ reliable observation
→ appropriate statistical model
→ causal or comparative evidence
→ bounded interpretation
</pre>

The stronger the inference beyond direct action, the more independent evidence is required.

